        ":question/problem": "The vectors are drawn on a square grid: $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/e9/e9a9adbc162bf6f73a5b2d058e4953b32528a6962a9b1c31ff7bef671e6c7c74.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "![Completed vector route](images/aa/aae1ea60df4eb3030354dc404cd0fc0670d46d46fa7ffe192129a30d5e59c77d.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $4$ squares left and $2$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares right and $1$ square down. Thus $OX=2|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-2\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $6$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=-3\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=-2\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}\\\\&=-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=-3$ and $\\mu=-2$."
